{"id": "145833", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=145833", "title": "Uzumqin", "text": "Uzumqin (\"East Ujimqin Banner\") is a small town in northern Inner Mongolia, China.\nUnderstand.\nIt is a typical small Inner Mongolian town with mostly new buildings, some of them quite soviet in style. It is very close to the south eastern borders of the country Mongolia and does not see a lot of tourists. It's likely that some of the locals will get interested and come up to talk to you and wish you welcome.\nGet in.\nThere are buses from Xilinhot and Beijing.\nGet around.\nBy foot, since it's so small, or by taxi if you want to travel a longer distance. \nSee.\nThe beautiful countryside and the Mongolian culture.\nBuy.\nThere are shopping opportunities along the main road, where you will find things such as saddles, high quality wool-clothes, traditional Mongolian dresses, traditional jewelry. The products are of course made in China, but not in the usual sense of the word: rather than cheap factory goods of dubious quality, they tend to be very well made - they're made to be used.\nEat.\nIt's easy to find small restaurants. Mostly Mongolian and Chinese dishes. Mongolian food puts a lot of emphasis on meat and diary-products. All of course from their own farm! Tourists with specific needs or preferences do best to bring their own food.\nDrink.\nMongolian tea and Baiju (strong alcoholic beverage) are two local drinks.\nSleep.\nThere are some small hotels, as everywhere in China."}
{"id": "145838", "revid": "162256", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=145838", "title": "Yangsan", "text": "Yangsan (\uc591\uc0b0\uc2dc) is a town to the west north of Busan in South Gyeongsang.\nUnderstand.\nYangsan is a new residential town for the larger city of Busan. It has an expanding population about 360,000. Historically known as a peaceful mountain community home to vital Buddhist treasures, modern Yangsan seamlessly blends centuries-old traditions with contemporary urban development.\nThe city is geographically defined by the Nakdong River to the west and scenic mountain ranges\u2014including Mt. Chobong and Mt. Yeongchuk\u2014to the north and east. Because of its proximity to Busan, Yangsan serves as a major commuter town, particularly around the sprawling Mulgeum New Town (\ubb3c\uae08\uc2e0\ub3c4\uc2dc), while retaining its own distinct industrial and cultural identity.\nFor travelers, Yangsan is most famous for Tongdosa Temple (\ud1b5\ub3c4\uc0ac), a UNESCO World Heritage site and one of Korea's \"Three Jewel Temples,\" renowned for harboring sacred relics of the Buddha without displaying a traditional Buddha statue in its main hall. Beyond its spiritual heritage, the city offers rich outdoor activities, including scenic hiking trails along the \"Alps of Southeastern Korea\" (Yeongnam Alps), the picturesque Naewonsa Valley(\ub0b4\uc6d0\uc0ac \uacc4\uace1), and beautiful plum blossoms in early spring at Wondong Station(\uc6d0\ub3d9\uc5ed).\nGet in.\nYangsan station is a terminus of the Busan subway line 2.\nThere are inter-city buses from Ulsan, Changwon, Gyeongju and other cities.\nBy train.\nTrains from Seoul Station to Mulgeum Station, such as Korail's KTX, ITX, and the Mugunghwa, run 14 to 15 times a day, taking about 2 hours and 40 minutes to 3 hours. This is the fastest way to get from Seoul to Yangsan. \nIf there is no direct KTX to Mulgeum Station, you can also take a route via Seoul Station \u2192 Ulsan Station \u2192 Yangsan. In this case, you should take bus 3000 from Ulsan Station to Yangsan Station."}
