{"id": "148210", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=148210", "title": "Hinsdale", "text": "Hinsdale is a village in Chicagoland.\nGet in.\nHinsdale is accessible by Metra train on the BNSF line. The main stop for downtown is Hinsdale, and the stops at Highlands and West Hinsdale are also in the town. \nBy car, Hinsdale is accessible by I-294 (exit Ogden Ave), IL Route 83, Ogden Ave, or 55th Street.\nBuy.\nDowntown Hinsdale has plenty of shopping."}
{"id": "148211", "revid": "2432955", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=148211", "title": "La Grange (Illinois)", "text": "La Grange is a village in Chicagoland.\nUnderstand.\nLa Grange, Illinois is a western suburb located in Cook County, about 13 to 14 miles west of downtown Chicago. It sits within Lyons Township and covers an area of roughly 2.5 square miles.\nHistory.\nThe area around La Grange was first settled in the 1830s, when Chicago residents moved out to the west due to the rapid population increase in the city in the decade since its incorporation. The first settler, Robert Leitch,came to the area in 1830, seven years before the City of Chicago was incorporated. La Grange's location, at approximately 13 miles (21 km) from the Chicago Loop.\nThere was a large spike in population in the 1880s and 1890s as the village grew from just over 500 to nearly 4,000 residents, over 600% growth in twenty years. The population continued to grow steadily through the 1960s, peaking at 17,814 according to the 1970 census.\nGet in.\nBy train.\nTake Metra BNSF to \"La Grange Road\" for downtown La Grange. There is also the stop \"La Grange Stone Avenue,\" which is west of downtown.\nBy car.\nOgden Ave or Mannheim/La Grange Rd.\nBy public transport.\nLa Grange area public transportation includes:\nEat.\nThere are three places to get frozen yogurt in downtown La Grange. "}
